\name{merge_clustirrs}
\alias{merge_clustirrs}
\title{Merge immune receptor repertoire similarity graphs}
\description{
\code{merge_clustirrs} merges the similarity graphs generated from multiple
immune receptor repertoire analyses into a single joint graph. Similarity
edges within each repertoire are retained, and additional similarity edges
are computed between repertoires for each available immune receptor chain.
}
\usage{
merge_clustirrs(x)
}
\arguments{
\item{x}{a list of objects returned by \code{\link{clustirr}}. Each element
must contain a \code{graph} object and the corresponding \code{clust_irrs}
results. The input objects must have been generated using identical control
parameters.}
}
\value{
The output is a list with the following elements.

\itemize{
\item{\code{graph}: an \code{igraph} object representing the joint similarity
graph. The graph contains the original within-repertoire edges together with
newly computed between-repertoire edges. The graph is undirected.}

\item{\code{multigraph}: logical variable indicating whether the resulting
graph is a joint graph composed of multiple repertoires. This value is
\code{TRUE} for the output of \code{merge_clustirrs}.}
}
}
\details{
\code{merge_clustirrs} combines similarity graphs generated independently
for multiple immune receptor repertoires.

The function performs the following steps.
\enumerate{
\item Checks that all input repertoires were analyzed using identical
control parameters. The function stops with an error if the control
parameters do not match.

\item Extracts the individual repertoire graphs from the input objects and
combines them using a disjoint union. This preserves the vertices and
within-repertoire similarity edges.

\item Identifies the immune receptor chains represented in the input graphs.

\item For every pair of repertoires and each available receptor chain,
computes similarities between CDR3 sequences from the two repertoires using
the same scoring procedure and control parameters used by
\code{\link{clustirr}}.

\item Adds the resulting between-repertoire similarity edges to the joint
graph. These edges are labeled with \code{type = "between-repertoire"}.

\item Removes duplicate edges connecting the same pair of vertices for the
same receptor chain.

\item Returns the resulting joint graph together with the
\code{multigraph} indicator.
}

CDR3 sequences with missing values (\code{NA}) are excluded from the
between-repertoire similarity calculations.

The resulting graph contains both within-repertoire and
between-repertoire similarity relationships. The edge attributes
\code{weight}, \code{cweight}, \code{nweight}, and \code{ncweight} retain
the similarity scores generated by the CDR3 similarity calculation.
\code{weight} and \code{cweight} represent scores for complete CDR3
sequences and their CDR3 cores, respectively, while \code{nweight} and
\code{ncweight} represent the corresponding normalized scores.

The input graphs must use identical control parameters because the
between-repertoire similarity calculations are performed using the common
control settings extracted from the input objects.

The function is intended to construct a joint similarity graph from
multiple independently analyzed repertoires, which can subsequently be used
for downstream graph-based analyses of immune receptor repertoire
similarities.
}
\examples{

\dontrun{

# Run clustirr separately for multiple repertoires

c1 <- clustirr(s = repertoire_A)
c2 <- clustirr(s = repertoire_B)
c3 <- clustirr(s = repertoire_C)

# Merge the repertoire graphs

merged <- merge_clustirrs(list(c1, c2, c3))

# Inspect the resulting graph

class(merged$graph)
summary(merged$graph)

# Check whether the graph is a multi-repertoire graph

merged$multigraph
}
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{clustirr}}
}
